{"answer":"There are 7 subsets: {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}. One example is {1,8}. Exhaustiveness follows because a sum-9 subset has at most three elements: four distinct positive elements have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. The two-element and three-element cases are completely enumerated below; the empty and one-element cases cannot sum to 9 from the given set.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify a subset summing to 9 by its cardinality, then enumerate strictly increasing positive tuples of the relevant lengths.","id":"cardinality-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This gives a finite exhaustive proof without relying on computation."},{"description":"Use the unrestricted partition number for 9 and filter to distinct parts at most 8.","id":"partition-filter","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It would require a separate partition enumeration and is less direct than the cardinality argument."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by exhaustive cardinality casework in steps cardinality-bound through count-total.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9.","step_ids":["cardinality-bound","one-element","two-element","three-element","count-total"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Listed and verified in steps two-element and three-element.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["count-seven"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The seven subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["two-element","three-element"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct verification in step example-subset.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["example-subset"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"cardinality-bound","justification":"Any four distinct positive integers have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Hence a sum-9 subset has at most three elements.","statement":"Only subset cardinalities 0, 1, 2, and 3 need be considered."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound"],"id":"one-element","justification":"The available elements are 1 through 8, so no one-element subset has sum 9; the empty subset has sum 0.","statement":"The cardinality-0 and cardinality-1 cases contribute no subsets."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound"],"id":"two-element","justification":"Write the elements as a<b with a+b=9. Then a<9/2, so a is 1, 2, 3, or 4, yielding respectively b=8, 7, 6, or 5. These are all valid and distinct.","statement":"The cardinality-2 case contributes exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["cardinality-bound"],"id":"three-element","justification":"Write the elements as a<b<c with a+b+c=9. Since a<b<c are positive, a can only be 1 or 2. If a=1, then b<c and b+c=8, so b=2 or 3, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}. If a=2, then b<c and b+c=7 with b>2, so b=3, giving {2,3,4}. No larger a is possible because 3+4+5>9.","statement":"The cardinality-3 case contributes exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["one-element","two-element","three-element"],"id":"count-total","justification":"The cardinality cases are disjoint. Their contributions are 0, 4, and 3 for cardinalities 1, 2, and 3, respectively, with 0 for cardinality 0; thus the total is 7.","statement":"The exhaustive total is 4+3=7."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"example-subset","justification":"Both 1 and 8 belong to the specified ground set, and 1+8=9.","statement":"The subset {1,8} has sum 9."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
